1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
In August of 2000, one of the world's great submarines glided through the frigid depths of an Arctic sea.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:12,000
She was there to practice the skills of a Cold War many thought was over.

3
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:17,000
But the demands of that war had planted the seeds of disaster in this great ship,

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:21,000
and 118 men would pay with their lives.

5
00:00:21,001 --> 00:00:25,000
Inviting Disaster, Kursk, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:42,657 --> 00:00:52,240
Submarine Kursk is really an illustration that these machines, very small quarters, very little space, very little room for error, are no place to cut corners.

7
00:00:54,820 --> 00:00:59,400
Saturday, August 12, 2000, 1120 a.m.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,640
the southern Barents Sea, north of Murmansk, Russia.

9
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:10,120
Even in summer, snow can fall on these waters that border the Kola Peninsula.

10
00:01:13,240 --> 00:01:18,280
But they are the only permanently ice-free access that Russia's northern fleet has to the world.

11
00:01:20,560 --> 00:01:25,360
And it is at this time of year that Russia conducts war games for the ships of this fleet.

12
00:01:25,361 --> 00:01:33,400
Today, the Russian cruiser Peter the Great leads other vessels of the fleet in a game

13
00:01:33,401 --> 00:01:36,400
of cat and mouse.

14
00:01:36,401 --> 00:01:41,480
Their opponent is not an American, but a Russian submarine, designated K-141.

15
00:01:41,481 --> 00:01:47,920
Her name is Kursk.

16
00:01:47,921 --> 00:01:52,480
The captain of the Kursk and his crew make preparations for a mock attack.

17
00:01:52,481 --> 00:01:54,480
is an attack that will never happen.

18
00:01:56,480 --> 00:02:02,480
At 11.28, an explosion equivalent to 200 pounds of TNT rocks the Kursk.

19
00:02:11,480 --> 00:02:13,480
65 miles away from Kursk,

20
00:02:13,481 --> 00:02:17,480
the American submarine Memphis lies eavesdropping on the Russian exercise.

21
00:02:17,481 --> 00:02:27,480
Only minutes after the first explosion, a second massive blast over 40 times greater than the first registers on the American sonar.

22
00:02:29,480 --> 00:02:32,480
We will never know for certain the cause of the explosions.

23
00:02:32,481 --> 00:02:40,480
What is known is that the enormous force of the second blast sends the Kursk and her crew to the bottom of the Barents Sea.

24
00:02:40,481 --> 00:02:44,480
It would be the Russian Navy's greatest tragedy.

25
00:02:44,481 --> 00:02:51,480
But the events that sank this great ship did not begin in these frigid waters or in the year 2000.

26
00:02:53,480 --> 00:02:56,480
Sometimes I'm asked when did the Kursk accident actually happen,

27
00:02:56,481 --> 00:03:00,480
and the easy answer is to say it happened on August 12, 2000.

28
00:03:00,481 --> 00:03:03,480
But actually the real answer is it happened ten years earlier.

29
00:03:03,481 --> 00:03:06,480
It happened when the Soviet Union collapsed.

30
00:03:06,481 --> 00:03:12,200
collapsed. The revolution in 1991 would bring freedoms that Russians had been

31
00:03:12,201 --> 00:03:16,520
dreaming of for decades, but it would also reveal that what had been the Soviet

32
00:03:16,521 --> 00:03:21,460
Union was on the brink of economic collapse. The Navy and its great

33
00:03:21,461 --> 00:03:27,700
submarine force were not immune to huge cutbacks. Life in the Soviet Navy had

34
00:03:27,701 --> 00:03:32,520
never been pampered, but sailors in the new Russian Navy now endured hardships

35
00:03:32,521 --> 00:03:33,920
never before asked of them.

36
00:03:36,060 --> 00:03:38,460
At its peak in the mid-1980s,

37
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:42,560
the Soviet submarine force deployed as many as 180 boats.

38
00:03:43,060 --> 00:03:47,040
But by the end of the 90s, fewer than 40 subs remained in service.

39
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:51,100
Northern ports were filled with rusting, abandoned submarines.

40
00:03:51,960 --> 00:03:53,820
Now, running those kind of systems,

41
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:58,360
when you don't have much in the way of financial backup and support,

42
00:03:58,361 --> 00:04:01,780
is really quite difficult, and it soon becomes potentially dangerous.

43
00:04:02,520 --> 00:04:03,960
They are high-tech beasts.

44
00:04:05,380 --> 00:04:11,720
But these guys were so, so transcendently patriotic about what they were doing for the motherland.

45
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:16,660
They were willing to defend it, and they were proud of defending it, regardless of what it took.

46
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:25,660
Even amidst the cutbacks, the seventh member of a new class of submarines is launched in May 1994.

47
00:04:26,540 --> 00:04:30,100
This newest sub is named for the largest tank battle of World War II.

48
00:04:30,101 --> 00:04:35,100
The enormous new boat is christened Kursk.

49
00:04:35,101 --> 00:04:45,100
The Kursk was 505 feet long, 60 feet across at the beam, and displaced 18,000 tons submerged.

50
00:04:45,101 --> 00:04:47,100
It was impressive, no question about it.

51
00:04:47,101 --> 00:04:55,100
We often look at the Russian Navy, the Russian Armed Forces, and believe it's a generation behind what the United States fields.

52
00:04:55,101 --> 00:04:59,100
But in fact, the Kursk was an exceptional piece of engineering.

53
00:05:00,100 --> 00:05:06,100
Kursk is five stories high and the length of two jumbo jets end to end.

54
00:05:06,101 --> 00:05:08,100
Her mess decks are spacious.

55
00:05:08,101 --> 00:05:12,100
Amazingly, she even has sauna baths and a swimming pool.

56
00:05:12,101 --> 00:05:17,100
To move all this through the water, Kursk is powered by two nuclear reactors

57
00:05:17,101 --> 00:05:21,100
that can propel her at almost 30 knots while submerged.

58
00:05:23,100 --> 00:05:29,100
In August 2000, the captain of this remarkable vessel is 45-year-old Gennady Lyatchin.

59
00:05:29,101 --> 00:05:35,100
He is a respected commander who is admired by his crew as fair and capable.

60
00:05:35,101 --> 00:05:41,100
As he prepares to launch his practice attack against the Peter the Great on that August day in 2000,

61
00:05:41,101 --> 00:05:45,100
Vyachin commands 117 men.

62
00:05:45,101 --> 00:05:49,100
Captain Lieutenant Dmitry Kolesnikov is typical of the men of the Kursk.

63
00:05:49,101 --> 00:05:56,100
The son of a submariner, Kolesnikov has been aboard Kursk for five years and loves his work.

64
00:05:56,101 --> 00:06:02,100
He had recently married and brought his new bride, Olga, to visit the ship he was so committed to.

65
00:06:03,100 --> 00:06:08,100
Olga and the rest of the world will never know exactly what happened on that day,

66
00:06:08,101 --> 00:06:11,100
but it is possible to reconstruct some of the events.

67
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:18,100
As Captain Liachin slowed to eight knots, he prepared to launch a practice version of the 6576 torpedo

68
00:06:18,101 --> 00:06:23,100
that Russian sailors nicknamed Fatty or the Fat Girl.

69
00:06:23,101 --> 00:06:29,020
The Fat Girl torpedo weighs 9,000 pounds and is propelled by kerosene fuel.

70
00:06:29,900 --> 00:06:32,420
But to move this enormous weapon at high speeds,

71
00:06:32,880 --> 00:06:36,700
the energy supplied to the motor by the kerosene must receive an immense boost

72
00:06:36,701 --> 00:06:39,260
by being force-fed huge amounts of oxygen.

73
00:06:40,980 --> 00:06:47,360
The oxygen is supplied by a simple but highly concentrated chemical called hydrogen peroxide.

74
00:06:47,361 --> 00:06:52,360
Hydrogen peroxide is a manufactured chemical.

75
00:06:52,361 --> 00:06:59,360
They start with water and then they put an extra oxygen atom on each water molecule.

76
00:06:59,361 --> 00:07:05,360
But if the hydrogen peroxide comes in contact with certain catalytic materials,

77
00:07:05,361 --> 00:07:08,360
such as copper or brass or other contaminants,

78
00:07:08,361 --> 00:07:13,360
then the hydrogen peroxide begins to disassociate rapidly.

79
00:07:13,361 --> 00:07:15,360
It produces great heat.

80
00:07:15,361 --> 00:07:22,360
The heat in turn generates great pressure by expanding the volume of peroxide by 5,000 times.

81
00:07:22,361 --> 00:07:28,360
But the Russians were willing to gamble on using this temperamental but powerful chemical.

82
00:07:28,361 --> 00:07:34,360
Both the British and American navies introduced peroxide torpedoes at the end of World War II,

83
00:07:34,361 --> 00:07:40,360
but by the 1960s, both navies abandoned peroxide as too dangerous.

84
00:07:40,361 --> 00:07:46,360
Peroxide-powered torpedoes required ongoing and highly skilled maintenance.

85
00:07:46,361 --> 00:07:49,360
Such maintenance can be demanding in the most well-funded navies.

86
00:07:49,361 --> 00:07:55,360
In the Russian Navy of 2000, peroxide torpedoes were accidents waiting to happen.

87
00:07:55,361 --> 00:08:05,360
Later review of records indicated the peroxide-fueled weapon that went into the Kursk's torpedo tube had not received the maintenance it required.

88
00:08:06,220 --> 00:08:12,200
It became clear that those torpedoes had been stored and forgotten.

89
00:08:13,000 --> 00:08:14,000
They had been neglected.

90
00:08:14,560 --> 00:08:19,180
And this is not a can of tomato soup that can be stored on a shelf for years.

91
00:08:20,760 --> 00:08:22,960
Sometime that morning of August 12th,

92
00:08:23,160 --> 00:08:27,700
the high-test peroxide probably managed to leak through a gasket or corroded seal.

93
00:08:28,460 --> 00:08:31,520
Once the leaking peroxide contacted brass or copper,

94
00:08:31,800 --> 00:08:34,960
pressure began to build with irresistible force.

95
00:08:35,360 --> 00:08:40,900
Until at last, the torpedo exploded like a balloon, backward into the torpedo room.

96
00:08:45,640 --> 00:08:50,460
The massive fireball raced through the first compartments, killing all in its path,

97
00:08:50,980 --> 00:08:52,880
probably including Captain Liocchin.

98
00:08:53,760 --> 00:08:58,240
Within seconds, temperatures in the torpedo room soared to 5,000 degrees

99
00:08:58,241 --> 00:09:01,820
until at least four torpedo warheads exploded.

100
00:09:01,821 --> 00:09:08,820
With the force of five tons of TNT, the blast obliterated the front of the Kursk.

101
00:09:08,821 --> 00:09:16,820
Slowly, the great ship headed down by the bow until she settled onto the bottom, 350 feet below the surface.

102
00:09:16,821 --> 00:09:23,820
The shockwave had moved back through the sub, battering down bulkheads and instantly killing all it hit.

103
00:09:23,821 --> 00:09:34,500
But buried in the belly of Kursk, the heavily reinforced nuclear reactor had stopped the force of the tremendous explosion.

104
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:39,060
After the reactor, four compartments remained unflooded.

105
00:09:39,680 --> 00:09:43,600
Huddled inside, 23 men now awaited their fate.

106
00:09:45,440 --> 00:09:48,260
Modern Marvels, Kursk, will return in a moment.

107
00:09:48,261 --> 00:09:54,760
We now return to Inviting Disaster, Kursk, on Modern Marvels.

108
00:09:55,900 --> 00:10:02,100
The explosions aboard the Kursk illustrate the principle that when human lives are at stake,

109
00:10:02,840 --> 00:10:10,140
need immediate help from wherever source, international, from experts around the world,

110
00:10:10,141 --> 00:10:11,060
from other governments.

111
00:10:11,540 --> 00:10:16,500
That is no time to be hiding, to be evasive,

112
00:10:17,260 --> 00:10:21,100
to be attempting to salvage a reputation.

113
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:26,840
August 12, 2000, sometime afternoon.

114
00:10:27,700 --> 00:10:29,480
Four of the rear compartments, of course,

115
00:10:29,580 --> 00:10:32,080
have survived the terrible explosions of that morning.

116
00:10:33,080 --> 00:10:34,780
In the red glow of emergency lighting,

117
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,380
survivors help each other toward the last compartment,

118
00:10:37,381 --> 00:10:43,260
where the air will soon become a stale atmosphere, rapidly filling with carbon dioxide.

119
00:10:44,260 --> 00:10:48,740
They are alive, but the cramped space in which they now huddle is miserable.

120
00:10:50,620 --> 00:10:53,840
It's extremely cold, it's extremely dark, they're very frightened,

121
00:10:54,460 --> 00:10:59,720
and they've probably got the feeling in the back of their mind that no one knows that it's happened to them.

122
00:11:01,540 --> 00:11:05,420
Like all modern subs, Kursk was equipped with two escape hatches.

123
00:11:05,421 --> 00:11:07,520
one has survived the blast.

124
00:11:10,520 --> 00:11:14,420
It is theoretically possible that the men can put on breathing masks

125
00:11:14,421 --> 00:11:15,720
and float to the surface.

126
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:21,380
But if no one picks them up immediately, they will die of hypothermia.

127
00:11:22,780 --> 00:11:24,180
There is another problem.

128
00:11:24,640 --> 00:11:26,640
Their small compartment is leaking.

129
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:33,060
The pressure of 350 feet of depth is forcing water into the sub.

130
00:11:33,061 --> 00:11:39,060
The worst is in the ninth compartment, which is the last in the submarine.

131
00:11:39,061 --> 00:11:44,060
It is here that the propulsion shafts go through the hull to the propellers.

132
00:11:44,061 --> 00:11:50,060
With no forward movement, the pressure has water shooting in around the shafts.

133
00:11:50,061 --> 00:11:53,060
But it isn't drowning that threatens the men.

134
00:11:53,061 --> 00:11:57,060
The problem wouldn't have been the water coming into the submarine.

135
00:11:57,061 --> 00:12:02,060
It's the fact that the water would cause the pressure to rise.

136
00:12:02,061 --> 00:12:09,380
As water fills the compartment, the remaining air surrounding the men would be compressed into higher and higher pressure.

137
00:12:10,540 --> 00:12:19,620
In any escape attempt, nitrogen forced into the crew's blood by the pressure would turn to bubbles as they ascended, twisting joints and making breathing impossible.

138
00:12:20,400 --> 00:12:26,220
Faced with frigid water and possibly dying of the bends, the men in compartment 9 make a choice.

139
00:12:27,020 --> 00:12:31,600
They decided that since they knew that the Northern Fleet had submarine rescue capabilities,

140
00:12:31,940 --> 00:12:33,160
they would wait for rescue.

141
00:12:33,800 --> 00:12:34,900
And who's to blame them?

142
00:12:35,780 --> 00:12:41,200
Captain Lieutenant Dmitry Kolesnikov has taken it upon himself to assume command of the survivors.

143
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:44,620
After the decision is made to stay in the Kursk,

144
00:12:45,120 --> 00:12:48,080
he manages to find paper and writes to his wife.

145
00:12:49,100 --> 00:12:51,320
Olga, I love you.

146
00:12:52,380 --> 00:12:54,220
Please, don't be too upset.

147
00:12:55,180 --> 00:12:56,520
It's 1316.

148
00:12:57,380 --> 00:13:01,700
All the crew from sections 6, 7, and 8 have moved to Section 9.

149
00:13:02,460 --> 00:13:03,760
There are 23 of us here.

150
00:13:04,280 --> 00:13:07,680
We have made this decision because none of us can escape.

151
00:13:10,140 --> 00:13:12,940
The problem with the survivor's decision to stay

152
00:13:12,941 --> 00:13:16,920
is that no one on the surface is taking any action to come to their aid.

153
00:13:17,940 --> 00:13:19,760
On board his flagship, Peter the Great,

154
00:13:20,280 --> 00:13:23,480
the commander of the Northern Fleet, Admiral Vyacheslav Popov,

155
00:13:23,481 --> 00:13:26,380
is aware Kursk is overdue to check in.

156
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:31,660
Now, he's told, two explosions have been picked up by sonar.

157
00:13:32,940 --> 00:13:37,760
As an ex-submariner, Popov knows something terrible has happened to Kursk.

158
00:13:38,520 --> 00:13:43,000
He also knows that as the fleet commander, he would take a great deal of the blame.

159
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:49,060
In the tradition of the Russian military, his immediate reaction on this day is to do nothing.

160
00:13:49,940 --> 00:13:53,460
Many people say that one of the greatest weaknesses of the Russian Northern Fleet,

161
00:13:53,560 --> 00:13:56,700
in fact of the Russian armed forces, is the flow of information.

162
00:13:57,280 --> 00:14:01,000
Very few people have an incentive to pass bad news up the chain of command.

163
00:14:02,160 --> 00:14:04,420
Popov finally notifies the high command,

164
00:14:04,920 --> 00:14:09,120
but it is not until 12 hours after the sinking that an emergency is declared.

165
00:14:10,600 --> 00:14:14,060
The Northern Fleet has rescued submersibles in nearby ports

166
00:14:14,061 --> 00:14:17,160
whose mission it is to rescue stranded submariners

167
00:14:17,161 --> 00:14:21,240
by diving down and locking onto the escape hatch of the stricken sub.

168
00:14:22,560 --> 00:14:25,100
One of these submersibles, the Priz,

169
00:14:25,300 --> 00:14:28,700
is finally loaded aboard a support vessel and put to sea.

170
00:14:29,100 --> 00:14:33,420
But Russian rescue vessels have also been the victims of Russian budget cuts.

171
00:14:36,860 --> 00:14:39,840
Sunday, August 13th, 5.20 p.m.

172
00:14:40,380 --> 00:14:43,140
Nearly 30 hours after the course went to the bottom,

173
00:14:43,800 --> 00:14:46,360
the Priz at last dives to attempt a rescue.

174
00:14:47,160 --> 00:14:52,000
The submersible relies on very crude thrusters to position her over the Kursk.

175
00:14:52,780 --> 00:14:55,660
Even worse, her batteries are hopelessly outdated

176
00:14:55,661 --> 00:14:58,880
and will allow only a short time to attempt the rescue.

177
00:15:00,060 --> 00:15:04,700
In spite of the limitations, the crew of the Priz come close to locking on to the Kursk.

178
00:15:05,500 --> 00:15:10,500
But their attempts fail, and they are forced to return to the surface to recharge batteries.

179
00:15:11,100 --> 00:15:14,780
It is sadly apparent that the little Russian sub is not equipped

180
00:15:14,781 --> 00:15:17,360
to bring the men of Kursk back to the surface.

181
00:15:17,860 --> 00:15:21,420
They were well-manned by very professional, very proud,

182
00:15:21,520 --> 00:15:23,120
in fact, very courageous men.

183
00:15:23,420 --> 00:15:26,200
But they couldn't fight the inadequate engineering,

184
00:15:26,480 --> 00:15:29,700
the inadequate repair work, the inadequate maintenance

185
00:15:29,701 --> 00:15:32,360
that hadn't been done for many, many years.

186
00:15:32,980 --> 00:15:37,120
But alternatives to the obsolete prees are available and standing by.

187
00:15:38,580 --> 00:15:41,180
Intelligence sectors of the British and American navies

188
00:15:41,181 --> 00:15:44,140
know full well that a Russian submarine has been lost.

189
00:15:44,780 --> 00:15:50,860
And both navies maintain excellent submarine rescue vessels ready to travel anywhere in the world at a moment's notice.

190
00:15:51,540 --> 00:15:54,840
The Russians know this, but they refuse to ask for help.

191
00:15:56,480 --> 00:16:02,540
For over 40 years, the British and American navies had been Russia's opponents in the game of the Cold War.

192
00:16:03,540 --> 00:16:10,320
To rush to ask Western navies for help now is more than the pride of Russian admirals can accept.

193
00:16:10,321 --> 00:16:16,940
some individuals who are still you know thinking that you know they're still spying on us they're

194
00:16:16,941 --> 00:16:23,200
still practicing with us as their as their enemy etc etc this is not a good time for us to get all

195
00:16:23,201 --> 00:16:29,860
kissy-faced what's fascinating to me is it is still the cold war the cold war is still very

196
00:16:29,861 --> 00:16:36,940
much alive in the kursk event it was probably never realistic that the russian navy would turn

197
00:16:36,941 --> 00:16:38,440
to the Americans for help.

198
00:16:38,900 --> 00:16:41,140
But the British Royal Navy does not come

199
00:16:41,141 --> 00:16:43,080
with nearly as much political baggage.

200
00:16:44,080 --> 00:16:46,180
And the British have two forms of technology

201
00:16:46,181 --> 00:16:48,480
that might save the survivors on the Kursk.

202
00:16:48,920 --> 00:16:51,600
The first is 1,500 miles away,

203
00:16:52,100 --> 00:16:54,020
sitting in a maintenance building in Scotland.

204
00:16:55,220 --> 00:16:56,520
This is LR-5.

205
00:16:56,720 --> 00:16:58,480
She weighs about 21 and a half tons,

206
00:16:58,960 --> 00:17:01,700
and she can carry 16 rescuees.

207
00:17:01,980 --> 00:17:03,260
She has a crew of three,

208
00:17:03,261 --> 00:17:08,160
and she's developed to go to depths of up to 400 meters.

209
00:17:09,160 --> 00:17:12,740
The LR-5 submersible is similar to the Russian Priz,

210
00:17:13,120 --> 00:17:15,680
but possesses much more advanced technology.

211
00:17:16,340 --> 00:17:19,960
Three zero feet, going off comms on finger search.

212
00:17:20,220 --> 00:17:23,700
Using sophisticated sonar, the LR-5 can be maneuvered

213
00:17:23,701 --> 00:17:27,260
to the escape hatch of a stricken sub up to 1,200 feet down.

214
00:17:27,840 --> 00:17:31,560
Unlike the Russian vessel, LR-5 can approach the stricken submarine

215
00:17:31,561 --> 00:17:32,880
from almost any angle.

216
00:17:33,260 --> 00:17:36,360
A flexible skirt settles over the escape hatch.

217
00:17:36,960 --> 00:17:41,500
Water inside the skirt is pumped out, sealing the LR-5 to the sub like a suction cup.

218
00:17:42,240 --> 00:17:46,200
The sub's hatch is opened, and the stranded crew can be taken aboard the rescue vessel.

219
00:17:49,480 --> 00:17:58,200
The offshore oil industry of Britain and Norway has been responsible for developing another technology that could be used to rescue the Gursk survivors.

220
00:17:58,201 --> 00:18:02,780
In order to construct and repair oil drilling equipment on the sea floor,

221
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:06,700
the oil industry has perfected the technique of saturation diving.

222
00:18:07,920 --> 00:18:12,860
The technique allows divers to work at deep ocean pressure down to 1,000 feet,

223
00:18:13,340 --> 00:18:16,660
then return to the surface without experiencing the bends.

224
00:18:17,880 --> 00:18:20,500
To do this, divers are placed in chambers,

225
00:18:20,820 --> 00:18:24,880
which increase the pressure on their bodies until it equals that of the ocean floor.

226
00:18:24,881 --> 00:18:28,880
The divers are then lowered to the bottom by a diving bell.

227
00:18:28,881 --> 00:18:34,880
When they have finished work for the day, they return to rest in the pressurized chamber.

228
00:18:34,881 --> 00:18:39,880
When their work is completely finished, the chamber slowly reduces the pressure on their bodies

229
00:18:39,881 --> 00:18:42,880
until it again matches the surface atmosphere.

230
00:18:42,881 --> 00:18:49,880
If saturation divers can reach the Kursk, perhaps they can establish contact with any survivors.

231
00:18:50,760 --> 00:18:55,260
Russia has very few such divers and no equipment to support such a complex mission.

232
00:18:55,840 --> 00:19:00,840
But in the days following the accident, the Russians see no reason to ask others to help.

233
00:19:04,100 --> 00:19:09,060
As a storm tosses the waters above the sunken Kursk, time is passing.

234
00:19:10,080 --> 00:19:12,320
The Russians have failed in their rescue attempts.

235
00:19:12,321 --> 00:19:16,740
The odds are increasingly against the 23 trapped survivors.

236
00:19:16,741 --> 00:19:23,780
The courage of the men in that compartment, as witnessed by the notes that were recovered

237
00:19:23,781 --> 00:19:27,300
that they had written to their families, was absolutely heroic.

238
00:19:28,300 --> 00:19:29,360
I don't know how they did it.

239
00:19:30,840 --> 00:19:36,660
Inside the dark and cold coffin of the ninth compartment, the surviving men are left with

240
00:19:36,661 --> 00:19:37,540
only flashlights.

241
00:19:38,480 --> 00:19:41,060
They gasp for breath as oxygen disappears.

242
00:19:42,060 --> 00:19:44,800
Dmitry Kolezhnikov writes again to his wife.

243
00:19:46,740 --> 00:19:48,680
None of us can escape to the surface.

244
00:19:49,620 --> 00:19:51,260
It seems like there are no chances.

245
00:19:52,200 --> 00:19:54,040
No more than 10 or 20 percent.

246
00:19:55,160 --> 00:19:57,380
Let's hope that at least someone will read this.

247
00:19:58,580 --> 00:19:59,700
Regards to everybody.

248
00:20:00,820 --> 00:20:01,840
No need to despair.

249
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:03,740
Kalishnikov.

250
00:20:06,920 --> 00:20:08,700
Your heart goes out to those guys.

251
00:20:09,620 --> 00:20:14,860
The Soviet Navy's first nuclear submarine was the K-3, the Leninsky Komsomol.

252
00:20:14,861 --> 00:20:17,600
It crossed the North Pole in 1962.

253
00:20:18,440 --> 00:20:19,940
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

254
00:20:21,820 --> 00:20:26,020
We now return to an inviting disaster, Kursk, on Modern Marvels.

255
00:20:26,840 --> 00:20:34,220
The Kursk disaster is only one in a long line of cases of misuse and under-respect of explosive materials.

256
00:20:35,040 --> 00:20:41,920
There's the explosion at the New London, Texas, School District, the main school building, in 1937,

257
00:20:41,921 --> 00:20:50,040
where just after school, almost 300 adults and children were caught up in the explosion of natural gas.

258
00:20:50,041 --> 00:20:53,700
And the second case that comes to mind is Texas City, Texas,

259
00:20:54,220 --> 00:21:02,500
where the Grand Camp, a Liberty ship full of 2,200 tons of ammonium nitrate, caught fire.

260
00:21:03,100 --> 00:21:06,100
The ship exploded, killing almost 500 people.

261
00:21:06,420 --> 00:21:10,560
So what we have here are cases where people were lulled into security,

262
00:21:10,561 --> 00:21:13,640
although they were dealing with large amounts of explosive materials,

263
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:16,160
and we would see that happen again with Kursk.

264
00:21:19,080 --> 00:21:21,900
Monday, August 14th, day three.

265
00:21:22,720 --> 00:21:25,660
Vladimir Putin, Russian president for just three months,

266
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:29,500
is on vacation at the Black Sea when Kursk goes to the bottom.

267
00:21:30,340 --> 00:21:33,300
He is not told of the accident until almost a day later.

268
00:21:33,960 --> 00:21:36,180
He accepts advice to remain on holiday.

269
00:21:36,780 --> 00:21:38,700
It is a decision he will regret.

270
00:21:40,560 --> 00:21:45,940
In England on this day, Commodore David Russell is the duty officer in charge of

271
00:21:45,941 --> 00:21:49,600
submarines for the Royal Navy when he is told of the Kursk.

272
00:21:49,601 --> 00:21:54,500
I think the first reaction as a submariner that I had that there was something

273
00:21:54,501 --> 00:21:59,040
wrong with another submarine was that really it's the same reaction that anyone

274
00:21:59,041 --> 00:22:03,020
who goes to sea has with any disaster at sea is that you really want to help if

275
00:22:03,021 --> 00:22:07,160
you can. I think that's particularly acute in the case of submariners. You know

276
00:22:07,161 --> 00:22:11,240
what it's going to be like on board and you know that time is of the essence.

277
00:22:11,241 --> 00:22:15,800
We don't need any orders in situations like that. We make up our minds and we

278
00:22:15,801 --> 00:22:21,900
get on with it. Without orders or authorization and still unrequested by

279
00:22:21,901 --> 00:22:26,560
Russia, Russell begins to set wheels in motion to come to the aid of possible

280
00:22:26,561 --> 00:22:32,420
survivors. He loads the LR-5 aboard a huge Antonov cargo jet which will carry

281
00:22:32,421 --> 00:22:36,700
the rescue submersible to Norway. There it will rendezvous with the

282
00:22:36,701 --> 00:22:38,700
mothership, the Normand Pioneer.

283
00:22:38,701 --> 00:22:43,880
You go with what you can and you get it to Norway, put it on board the mothership and

284
00:22:43,881 --> 00:22:49,460
send the ship towards Russian waters because you can always bring it back.

285
00:22:49,461 --> 00:22:57,020
By noon on Monday, the British Embassy in Moscow officially offers help with the rescue.

286
00:22:57,021 --> 00:22:59,480
American officials will soon do the same.

287
00:22:59,481 --> 00:23:05,240
But in Russia, military leaders are still pretending that all is under control.

288
00:23:05,241 --> 00:23:07,920
are flying everywhere about the loss of the Kursk.

289
00:23:08,360 --> 00:23:11,140
Instead of addressing the rumors with directness and honesty,

290
00:23:11,680 --> 00:23:14,760
the Russian military resorts to the deceptions of the past.

291
00:23:15,360 --> 00:23:18,340
The Kursk is described as having simply a malfunction.

292
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:23,480
Admiral Popov offers the preposterous claim

293
00:23:23,481 --> 00:23:25,900
that communications have been established with the crew

294
00:23:25,901 --> 00:23:28,640
and that water and oxygen are being supplied.

295
00:23:29,840 --> 00:23:32,880
It is poignant to think that as they were dying,

296
00:23:33,600 --> 00:23:34,920
there in the ninth compartment,

297
00:23:34,921 --> 00:23:41,280
their families ashore were being comprehensively lied to by the admirals that those 23 men were serving.

298
00:23:42,440 --> 00:23:51,060
As hours turn to days, media pressure at home and internationally begins to build for President Putin to intervene in Kursk operations.

299
00:23:52,320 --> 00:23:55,680
It is no longer the dark Soviet days of restricted press.

300
00:23:56,360 --> 00:24:03,500
Now reporters want to know why Putin is still on holiday and why the names of those aboard Kursk have yet to be released.

301
00:24:03,501 --> 00:24:08,220
He'd only been in power, remember, 100 days at this point.

302
00:24:08,221 --> 00:24:12,660
He didn't understand that he had to be seen to be helping, that he couldn't just delegate

303
00:24:12,661 --> 00:24:16,500
this to submarine rescue professionals up there on the Kola Peninsula, that he had to

304
00:24:16,501 --> 00:24:18,060
get personally involved.

305
00:24:18,061 --> 00:24:23,200
Finally, the Russian president begins to grasp the potential political consequences

306
00:24:23,201 --> 00:24:26,260
of the unraveling story of Kursk.

307
00:24:26,261 --> 00:24:31,540
After a call from President Bill Clinton urging him to act, Putin orders his admirals to accept

308
00:24:31,541 --> 00:24:33,140
Western aid in the rescue.

309
00:24:35,280 --> 00:24:37,120
One of the first things requested

310
00:24:37,121 --> 00:24:39,400
are divers to go down to the Kursk

311
00:24:39,401 --> 00:24:41,280
to see if they can get any response

312
00:24:41,281 --> 00:24:42,500
from inside the vessel.

313
00:24:43,280 --> 00:24:44,860
The call for help goes to Stolt,

314
00:24:45,260 --> 00:24:47,020
a Norwegian and British underwater

315
00:24:47,021 --> 00:24:49,060
construction company for the oil industry.

316
00:24:50,240 --> 00:24:51,940
One of the first calls they make

317
00:24:51,941 --> 00:24:53,560
is to divert Tony Scott.

318
00:24:54,780 --> 00:24:57,120
Then when the phone call came from Stolt offshore,

319
00:24:57,980 --> 00:24:58,680
they were a bit cagy.

320
00:24:58,681 --> 00:25:00,620
They just had a deep diving job

321
00:25:00,621 --> 00:25:01,321
in northern Norway.

322
00:25:01,540 --> 00:25:07,040
I knew what it was because, you know, I was expecting something and just sort of jumped at the opportunity.

323
00:25:08,900 --> 00:25:16,760
Tony Scott and the other saturation divers will meet the dive ship Seaway Eagle in Tromso, Norway, where it will be rigged for the mission.

324
00:25:17,520 --> 00:25:20,020
In less than 12 hours, the Eagle heads north.

325
00:25:20,500 --> 00:25:24,440
She is followed by the Normand Pioneer carrying the LR-5 rescue sub.

326
00:25:25,940 --> 00:25:29,860
Commodore David Russell will be overall coordinating officer for the Royal Navy.

327
00:25:29,861 --> 00:25:32,660
What he needs now are technical answers.

328
00:25:33,260 --> 00:25:36,740
We need technical information because this is a complex operation.

329
00:25:37,480 --> 00:25:40,600
You need to know what the situation is actually on site.

330
00:25:41,340 --> 00:25:44,580
But Russian authorities are not quick to provide such information.

331
00:25:45,360 --> 00:25:46,900
It is clear that they are stalling.

332
00:25:47,760 --> 00:25:50,040
The British rescue team heads toward the Kursk,

333
00:25:50,440 --> 00:25:52,680
knowing little more than what has appeared in the press.

334
00:25:53,500 --> 00:25:56,480
As the British ships steam north at full speed,

335
00:25:56,481 --> 00:25:59,480
Russia's Deputy Prime Minister, Ilya Klebanov,

336
00:25:59,481 --> 00:26:03,480
meets with the families and attempts to dodge responsibility for the accident.

337
00:26:03,481 --> 00:26:08,480
Without any evidence, he claims the Kursk was sunk by an American submarine.

338
00:26:08,481 --> 00:26:11,480
Nadezhda Tilyk has a son aboard Kursk.

339
00:26:11,481 --> 00:26:14,480
At last, her fear and frustration with such lies

340
00:26:14,481 --> 00:26:17,480
move her to interrupt the Deputy Prime Minister

341
00:26:17,481 --> 00:26:20,480
and shout what so many families are feeling.

342
00:26:26,480 --> 00:26:32,760
In a scene that shocks the world, a female medic steps forward with a hypodermic needle containing sedatives.

343
00:26:33,280 --> 00:26:36,580
She injects Nadezhda Tilik through her coat into her thigh.

344
00:26:42,820 --> 00:26:46,000
Tilik sinks into her chair as the sedative takes effect.

345
00:26:46,920 --> 00:26:51,400
Russia is changing, but some of the old ways of dealing with dissent still remain.

346
00:26:51,401 --> 00:26:57,880
Saturday, August 19th, 11.30 a.m.

347
00:26:58,420 --> 00:27:01,100
The Normand pioneer arrives at the site of the accident.

348
00:27:01,620 --> 00:27:02,400
Hopes are high.

349
00:27:03,060 --> 00:27:04,360
The Kursk is a huge ship.

350
00:27:04,860 --> 00:27:07,680
A sealed compartment would hold a large quantity of air,

351
00:27:08,260 --> 00:27:10,960
perhaps enough that men could still be alive.

352
00:27:11,600 --> 00:27:14,900
But the English and Norwegian rescue team is greeted with a shock

353
00:27:14,901 --> 00:27:17,160
when Russian admirals arrive for a meeting.

354
00:27:18,080 --> 00:27:21,100
Russian search and rescue commander Admiral Gennady Verich

355
00:27:21,101 --> 00:27:27,240
amazingly tells Commodore Russell that the LR-5 will have to stay miles away from the Kursk.

356
00:27:28,080 --> 00:27:30,800
The rescue team begins to wonder why they'd come.

357
00:27:31,840 --> 00:27:37,220
I think the problem was we rapidly began to get the feeling that actually Admiral Verichel's agenda was one of,

358
00:27:37,600 --> 00:27:42,500
they're probably dead already, we just need to bring this to a quick close by finding that out.

359
00:27:42,501 --> 00:27:45,200
We know as submariners what it would be like to be in that position.

360
00:27:45,320 --> 00:27:48,600
You really wouldn't like someone giving up after four or five days,

361
00:27:48,680 --> 00:27:49,960
maybe because that's what the book said.

362
00:27:49,961 --> 00:27:53,480
you'd want them to continue until there was absolutely no chance.

363
00:27:54,500 --> 00:27:59,220
The British and Norwegian team, the best hope for rescue, is at last on the scene.

364
00:27:59,660 --> 00:28:05,960
But it appears they will not be allowed to give the men of Kursk that one last chance for survival.

365
00:28:06,920 --> 00:28:07,620
The U.S.

366
00:28:07,621 --> 00:28:12,940
Navy's first use of a rescue craft occurred after the sinking of the Squalus in 1939.

367
00:28:13,840 --> 00:28:15,260
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

368
00:28:15,261 --> 00:28:22,080
We now return to Inviting Disaster, Kursk, on Modern Marvels.

369
00:28:23,200 --> 00:28:28,080
One way to think of it is that so much effort goes into developing these machines,

370
00:28:28,520 --> 00:28:32,720
and it seems to suck up the money and the time and the expertise,

371
00:28:34,040 --> 00:28:40,380
leaving the tail end for working out the kinks, for writing the manuals,

372
00:28:40,381 --> 00:28:42,460
for working through the procedures.

373
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:44,840
What if X happens?

374
00:28:44,841 --> 00:28:47,440
what are the people on the scene supposed to do?

375
00:28:50,200 --> 00:28:53,660
Sunday, August 20th, 10 a.m., the ninth day.

376
00:28:54,640 --> 00:28:56,600
After traveling over 1,500 miles,

377
00:28:56,980 --> 00:29:01,600
the LR-5 and its crew are now forced to sit helplessly miles from the Kursk.

378
00:29:02,480 --> 00:29:05,020
Russian Admiral Gennady Verich won't admit it,

379
00:29:05,420 --> 00:29:09,000
but he has never wanted foreigners involved in the Kursk tragedy.

380
00:29:09,600 --> 00:29:14,740
He just wants to get the situation resolved, good or bad.

381
00:29:14,840 --> 00:29:16,080
but without foreigners.

382
00:29:16,480 --> 00:29:19,560
Dead guys equals not so great,

383
00:29:19,561 --> 00:29:22,760
but dead guys with foreigners equals, you know,

384
00:29:22,820 --> 00:29:24,300
sort of a political living hell.

385
00:29:25,320 --> 00:29:28,960
But the Russians do finally authorize the divers from Seaway Eagle

386
00:29:28,961 --> 00:29:30,640
to go down to Kursk.

387
00:29:32,060 --> 00:29:34,780
Tony Scott and the other Norwegian and British divers

388
00:29:34,781 --> 00:29:38,240
have been in the compression chamber for hours preparing for the descent.

389
00:29:39,260 --> 00:29:42,140
Now they enter the diving bell and head for the bottom.

390
00:29:42,141 --> 00:29:51,880
I think everybody was sort of thinking that it wasn't 100% likely that there was going to be any survivors, but there was just that chance.

391
00:29:52,500 --> 00:29:57,340
Scott leaves the diving bell and enters the cold, pitch-dark world of the ocean floor.

392
00:29:58,120 --> 00:30:03,320
To maintain body heat at this depth, hot water is constantly pumped into his suit.

393
00:30:04,560 --> 00:30:10,640
There's a very big, prominent white painted ring around the hatch, and that came into view.

394
00:30:10,641 --> 00:30:14,020
You know, I had imagined it to be a rounded shape,

395
00:30:14,100 --> 00:30:16,460
and you might have to cling on to it and tie yourself off,

396
00:30:16,660 --> 00:30:18,260
but you're just flat.

397
00:30:19,180 --> 00:30:21,920
It's just like standing on top of a building or something.

398
00:30:22,780 --> 00:30:26,780
After a few minutes, dive supervisors above pass the word.

399
00:30:27,180 --> 00:30:32,100
It is time to send a signal to anyone who might still be alive inside the Korsk.

400
00:30:32,800 --> 00:30:36,140
Scott will tap a prearranged code on the side of the hull.

401
00:30:37,400 --> 00:30:39,300
There's no doubt it will be heard inside.

402
00:30:39,301 --> 00:30:44,880
and what we were desperately hoping to hear was a return tap.

403
00:30:45,960 --> 00:30:48,960
Every person on the eagle was awake,

404
00:30:49,240 --> 00:30:51,720
the whole ship was holding its breath and hoping for the best.

405
00:31:04,200 --> 00:31:07,280
We did want it to be, you know, if you could will something to happen,

406
00:31:07,281 --> 00:31:08,720
that's what he wanted to happen.

407
00:31:08,780 --> 00:31:09,640
He wanted to hear something.

408
00:31:10,780 --> 00:31:12,580
Tony rests his head on the hole

409
00:31:12,581 --> 00:31:16,000
and stops breathing to listen for the slightest response.

410
00:31:17,240 --> 00:31:19,560
Sadly, there is no answer.

411
00:31:22,680 --> 00:31:24,820
But the lack of response to the tapping

412
00:31:24,821 --> 00:31:27,760
still doesn't rule out the chance of life inside.

413
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:31,220
Not having gained any response,

414
00:31:31,460 --> 00:31:34,520
that didn't necessarily mean there was nobody alive.

415
00:31:34,620 --> 00:31:35,480
You know, they could have been unconscious,

416
00:31:35,481 --> 00:31:37,820
They could have been in stupor or coma or whatever.

417
00:31:38,540 --> 00:31:43,900
The priority for the divers now shifts to finding out if the compartment below is flooded.

418
00:31:45,480 --> 00:31:52,820
The only means of getting at the sub-interior is through the remaining escape hatch Scott and his diving partner are hovering over.

419
00:31:54,100 --> 00:32:01,340
The hatch leads to an intermediate chamber, which is separated from the ninth compartment by a second lower hatch that opens inward.

420
00:32:01,341 --> 00:32:06,280
If the divers open the outer hatch and the inner hatch is already open,

421
00:32:06,880 --> 00:32:08,400
survivors below will drown.

422
00:32:09,320 --> 00:32:13,120
It is vital the rescuers learn if the interconnecting chamber is flooded.

423
00:32:13,860 --> 00:32:18,500
They come up with a plan to do this by opening a small valve on the outer hatch.

424
00:32:19,320 --> 00:32:22,360
There's what we call an equalization valve.

425
00:32:22,361 --> 00:32:26,940
You operate and it tells you whether you're sucking water in

426
00:32:26,941 --> 00:32:33,300
or whether there's no suction and therefore inside and outside are equal pressure.

427
00:32:34,780 --> 00:32:39,280
If the chamber below is dry, water around the valve will be sucked inward

428
00:32:39,281 --> 00:32:41,560
because the ocean pressure is so much greater.

429
00:32:42,440 --> 00:32:46,560
If the chamber below is flooded, the pressure is equal and no water will move.

430
00:32:47,260 --> 00:32:48,280
The difference is critical.

431
00:32:49,260 --> 00:32:52,800
The problem is it is almost impossible to observe the water's movement.

432
00:32:53,200 --> 00:32:56,840
The engineers aboard the Seaway Eagle devise a simple test.

433
00:32:56,940 --> 00:33:01,460
So what we did is we had a bottle made up with, I believe it was milk at the time,

434
00:33:01,540 --> 00:33:04,520
some substance that was easily seen underwater.

435
00:33:04,840 --> 00:33:07,260
And we just squirted it around the valve.

436
00:33:07,820 --> 00:33:10,400
You know, you would see that flowing in if it was going in the seawater.

437
00:33:11,480 --> 00:33:13,660
There was no detectable flow in or out.

438
00:33:14,500 --> 00:33:19,200
That was pretty good evidence that that hatch, below that hatch, was flooded.

439
00:33:21,280 --> 00:33:24,960
The outer hatch is opened, showing the chamber is indeed flooded.

440
00:33:24,961 --> 00:33:30,880
There is virtually no hope remaining for the survivors, but the lower hatch will have to be opened.

441
00:33:31,700 --> 00:33:36,360
A special wrench is manufactured aboard the Eagle and sent down to the divers.

442
00:33:37,320 --> 00:33:38,900
The Russians asked us to open the hatch.

443
00:33:40,080 --> 00:33:47,560
So we set the hatch dogging mechanism to the open position, but still the hatch didn't drop open.

444
00:33:49,000 --> 00:33:53,440
Although not yet fully open, the lower hatch seal has now been cracked.

445
00:33:54,260 --> 00:33:57,060
Bubbles pulse around the seal from the compartment below.

446
00:33:58,060 --> 00:34:03,140
They cause some confusion until someone realizes it is simply the surge of the tide

447
00:34:03,141 --> 00:34:06,100
pulling small amounts of air out of the ninth compartment.

448
00:34:06,940 --> 00:34:09,880
The time has arrived to fully open the inner hatch.

449
00:34:10,460 --> 00:34:13,020
An arm of the remote vehicle pushes it open.

450
00:34:16,900 --> 00:34:21,420
The remaining gas that had accumulated in the ninth compartment rushes out.

451
00:34:21,421 --> 00:34:26,420
Later tests will confirm this gas was unfit for human life.

452
00:34:26,421 --> 00:34:30,420
As the bubbles stream upward, then burst and disappear,

453
00:34:30,421 --> 00:34:34,420
so too do the remaining hopes for the men of Kursk.

454
00:34:37,420 --> 00:34:43,420
It was a remarkable display of commitment by the Norwegian and the British rescuers

455
00:34:43,421 --> 00:34:45,420
who went to the scene that August 2000.

456
00:34:45,421 --> 00:34:49,420
They didn't give a damn about the politics in the Ministry of Defence.

457
00:34:49,421 --> 00:34:52,420
They didn't give a damn about the Russian admirals.

458
00:34:52,421 --> 00:34:56,420
They were genuinely motivated by a desire to save human life.

459
00:34:58,420 --> 00:35:02,420
The work of the British and Norwegian rescue specialists is finished.

460
00:35:02,421 --> 00:35:05,420
They will not participate in any salvage effort.

461
00:35:06,420 --> 00:35:08,420
For the first time since it arrived,

462
00:35:08,421 --> 00:35:12,420
the Normand pioneer is allowed to float directly over the Kursk.

463
00:35:13,420 --> 00:35:15,420
On behalf of the Royal Navy,

464
00:35:15,421 --> 00:35:18,420
Commodore Russell leads a memorial service.

465
00:35:18,421 --> 00:35:22,420
and flowers are tossed to float above the dead submarine.

466
00:35:22,421 --> 00:35:25,420
We didn't feel we could have done anything better than we did.

467
00:35:25,421 --> 00:35:28,420
It's about the only comfort there is to take from it.

468
00:35:28,421 --> 00:35:34,420
Except perhaps that it may just be that out of these tragic circumstances

469
00:35:34,421 --> 00:35:37,420
comes a little bit of hope for the future.

470
00:35:37,421 --> 00:35:41,420
All attempts to rescue the men of Kursk have failed,

471
00:35:41,421 --> 00:35:46,420
but the impact of this disaster on a grieving Russia is not over.

472
00:35:48,420 --> 00:35:58,460
Energetic, oxygen-containing chemicals have been implicated in non-submarine accidents also, including the crash of Valuejet Flight 592 in 1996.

473
00:35:59,400 --> 00:36:00,820
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

474
00:36:01,940 --> 00:36:06,120
We now return to an inviting disaster, Kursk, on Modern Marvels.

475
00:36:06,740 --> 00:36:14,140
We tend to relate most to people like ourselves, who are bystanders, who are accidentally caught up in these events.

476
00:36:14,141 --> 00:36:21,200
But so often, it's the workers that really pay the price for what I regard as carelessness at the top.

477
00:36:22,320 --> 00:36:27,120
That the management does not have the proper attitude of the healthy fear.

478
00:36:30,060 --> 00:36:32,140
August 22nd, 2000.

479
00:36:33,620 --> 00:36:36,860
Two days after all hope is given up for the men of Kursk,

480
00:36:37,460 --> 00:36:40,780
Russian President Vladimir Putin at last arrives in Vidyaivo,

481
00:36:40,781 --> 00:36:45,040
the home port of the sub, to meet with the families of the dead crew.

482
00:36:46,640 --> 00:36:51,240
He pledges that Kursk will be raised and the bodies of their loved ones retrieved.

483
00:36:52,380 --> 00:36:55,520
Although Putin's pledge is well received by the grieving families,

484
00:36:56,900 --> 00:37:00,360
accomplishing the feat will be an enormous technological challenge.

485
00:37:02,760 --> 00:37:07,800
Two Dutch companies are contracted to take on the immense job of retrieving the great sub.

486
00:37:08,680 --> 00:37:11,660
Work begins 11 months after the accident.

487
00:37:12,340 --> 00:37:13,860
The forward section of Kursk,

488
00:37:14,140 --> 00:37:16,700
which had sustained massive damage from the explosions,

489
00:37:17,120 --> 00:37:18,800
will be cut off and left behind.

490
00:37:19,500 --> 00:37:22,620
A diamond-studded chain acts like an immense hacksaw

491
00:37:22,621 --> 00:37:25,500
as it slices through the double hull of high-strength steel.

492
00:37:26,420 --> 00:37:28,520
Working in the tremendous tangle of debris

493
00:37:28,521 --> 00:37:32,000
and cutting into unknown spaces is very dangerous.

494
00:37:32,001 --> 00:37:38,000
My personal thoughts are cutting into any space is hazardous

495
00:37:38,001 --> 00:37:43,000
because you get a buildup of hydrogen and oxygen in a concentration that is very explosive.

496
00:37:43,001 --> 00:37:48,000
There have been divers killed by explosions by cutting into things due to the buildup of the gases.

497
00:37:50,000 --> 00:37:56,000
Once the bow of Korsk has been cut off, a huge barge, appropriately named Giant,

498
00:37:56,001 --> 00:37:59,000
is towed from Holland and positioned over the sub.

499
00:37:59,001 --> 00:38:07,840
the sub. Giant is equipped with 26 heavy cables, each capable of lifting 900 tons.

500
00:38:07,841 --> 00:38:13,180
Divers begin cutting holes into the side of Kursk with powerful jets of abrasive

501
00:38:13,181 --> 00:38:15,520
sand.

502
00:38:15,880 --> 00:38:22,600
After weeks of work each of the cables on the barge is attached to the holes.

503
00:38:22,601 --> 00:38:27,600
On October 8, 2001, 14 months after the accident,

504
00:38:27,601 --> 00:38:32,600
mammoth jacks on board the giant at last begin to lift the Kursk

505
00:38:32,601 --> 00:38:35,600
and the remains of the sailors who rest within her.

506
00:38:37,600 --> 00:38:42,600
Even without her bow, Kursk weighs 9,600 tons.

507
00:38:43,600 --> 00:38:47,600
Never before has anything this heavy been lifted from the bottom of the sea.

508
00:38:47,601 --> 00:38:50,700
of the sea.

509
00:38:50,701 --> 00:38:56,280
It takes 15 hours to raise the hulk into position below the giant.

510
00:38:56,281 --> 00:39:02,780
Ocean-going tugs then begin to tow the barge and its grim cargo to a waiting dry dock in

511
00:39:02,781 --> 00:39:05,320
Murmansk.

512
00:39:05,321 --> 00:39:10,920
As Kursk leaves the scene of its death, divers place a marble tombstone on the floor of the

513
00:39:10,921 --> 00:39:15,920
sea.

514
00:39:15,921 --> 00:39:24,480
Suspended beneath the barge, Kursk is at last towed into position in the dry dock.

515
00:39:24,481 --> 00:39:31,200
On October 23rd, 2001, water is drained and the face of the great submarine once again

516
00:39:31,201 --> 00:39:35,480
appears.

517
00:39:35,481 --> 00:39:40,600
In the weeks that follow, bodies are removed and investigators pour over every part of

518
00:39:40,601 --> 00:39:42,400
the sub.

519
00:39:42,401 --> 00:39:46,240
Three of the 118 crewmen are left unidentified.

520
00:39:47,520 --> 00:39:51,780
Autopsies of the dead sailors reveal the 23 survivors in the ninth compartment

521
00:39:51,781 --> 00:39:55,500
had last met their end in a second disaster.

522
00:39:55,501 --> 00:40:00,840
The final event that killed those sailors was a fire, an intense fire,

523
00:40:01,220 --> 00:40:04,900
caused by their chemical canisters that generated oxygen

524
00:40:04,901 --> 00:40:10,940
meeting an oily film on the water that was rising in that compartment.

525
00:40:10,941 --> 00:40:16,940
Each compartment of Kursk was equipped with canisters holding potassium superoxide.

526
00:40:17,740 --> 00:40:22,080
The chemical was used to scrub the air of carbon dioxide and replace oxygen.

527
00:40:22,720 --> 00:40:28,960
Unfortunately, if potassium superoxide touches water, it reacts violently, releasing great heat.

528
00:40:29,780 --> 00:40:33,900
At some time, one of the struggling survivors probably dropped one of these canisters.

529
00:40:34,680 --> 00:40:39,500
Its heat and high oxygen content would have turned the oily water into a blowtorch.

530
00:40:40,940 --> 00:40:47,840
Those who didn't die in the fire suffocated when the last of the oxygen was consumed by the flames.

531
00:40:48,840 --> 00:40:56,420
It now appears certain that all were dead well before the British and Norwegians received the call to come to their aid.

532
00:40:59,760 --> 00:41:08,740
The investigative board at last confirms leaking peroxide in the Fat Girl torpedo as the cause of the explosions which killed Kursk.

533
00:41:08,741 --> 00:41:11,400
but that answer does not go far enough.

534
00:41:12,620 --> 00:41:15,440
The men of Kursk gave their lives

535
00:41:15,441 --> 00:41:18,720
because of Russia's ongoing but unrealistic commitment

536
00:41:18,721 --> 00:41:22,340
to a level of military technology it could no longer afford.

537
00:41:23,420 --> 00:41:25,980
Not only the technology of their torpedoes,

538
00:41:26,460 --> 00:41:29,460
but that of their rescue vessels had been allowed to fail

539
00:41:29,461 --> 00:41:33,780
by a system that deluded itself into thinking it was still a superpower.

540
00:41:33,781 --> 00:41:43,780
The tragedy of Kursk reminds the world that arrogance and pride can be killers when mixed with high technology.

541
00:41:43,781 --> 00:41:51,560
I believe that history is going to view it as the last dramatic event of the Cold War.

542
00:41:51,640 --> 00:41:53,920
Those guys died for the Cold War.

543
00:41:54,160 --> 00:41:57,780
We've thought of that Cold War as one in which there was very little bloodshed.

544
00:41:58,560 --> 00:42:03,480
Well, there's 118 guys and a lot of wrecked families who can testify otherwise.

545
00:42:03,780 --> 00:42:11,500
Vladimir Putin survived the criticism of his slow response to the Kursk tragedy, but he

546
00:42:11,501 --> 00:42:15,900
had tasted the public's anger as had no Russian leader since the Tsar.

547
00:42:15,901 --> 00:42:18,860
Now, Putin demands reform.

548
00:42:18,861 --> 00:42:23,080
Leaders who had put their careers and self-interest in the way of prompt reporting and rescue

549
00:42:23,081 --> 00:42:25,020
are called to account.

550
00:42:25,021 --> 00:42:31,100
Rear Admiral Verich, who had refused to allow LR-5 to even enter the water, was fired.

551
00:42:31,101 --> 00:42:35,980
Popov, who had resisted taking action as men died, was relieved of command.

552
00:42:37,940 --> 00:42:43,480
Ilya Klebanov, the deputy premier who had steadfastly insisted Kursk had been sunk by

553
00:42:43,481 --> 00:42:45,860
an American submarine, was fired.

554
00:42:48,880 --> 00:42:55,260
But Nadezhda Tilik, who had screamed in rage at Klebanov, became a hero to all who dream

555
00:42:55,261 --> 00:42:58,400
of being able to criticize their government without fear.

556
00:42:58,401 --> 00:43:06,780
The story of failure in Russia's once proud submarine fleet unfortunately continues.

557
00:43:06,781 --> 00:43:15,580
On August 30th, 2003, the sub K159 sank as it was being towed to a scrapyard.

558
00:43:15,581 --> 00:43:19,880
Nine men died in a tragedy described as needless.

559
00:43:19,881 --> 00:43:24,180
This time President Putin responded immediately to the news of the sinking and launched another

560
00:43:24,181 --> 00:43:26,700
investigation.

561
00:43:26,701 --> 00:43:31,920
Russian sailors had once again paid with their lives for the inept decision-making of their

562
00:43:31,921 --> 00:43:33,189
commanders.

563
00:43:33,190 --> 00:43:42,880
As the months passed, Russians paused to bury and grieve for the men who died aboard

564
00:43:42,881 --> 00:43:43,581
Kursk.

565
00:43:43,582 --> 00:43:49,120
They had given their lives to Mother Russia, not on a battlefield, but in a submarine

566
00:43:49,121 --> 00:43:55,243
made dangerous by outmoded equipment, poor maintenance, and shoddy standards.

567
00:43:55,244 --> 00:43:55,944
But the

568
00:43:55,945 --> 00:44:02,800
The deaths of the men of Kursk marked a very real precedent in Russia's evolving democracy.

569
00:44:02,801 --> 00:44:07,840
It revealed a shift in the way Russia's people respond to the deceit and manipulation of

570
00:44:07,841 --> 00:44:09,740
their government.

571
00:44:09,741 --> 00:44:14,200
We often talk about how the cursed submarine is a metaphor for Russia in decline, but the

572
00:44:14,201 --> 00:44:19,180
families are also a metaphor for a new Russia, for a more hopeful Russia, because they understand

573
00:44:19,181 --> 00:44:22,740
that they're no longer prepared to be lied to without reacting.

574
00:44:22,741 --> 00:44:27,040
They now understand they have a voice in a new Russia.

575
00:44:27,041 --> 00:44:32,500
Kursk was eventually towed to a scrapyard where she was chopped into pieces and melted

576
00:44:32,501 --> 00:44:33,500
down.

577
00:44:33,501 --> 00:44:37,220
But the great submarine did leave something of herself behind.

578
00:44:37,221 --> 00:44:43,240
The drama of the men who fought for life within her hull continues to provide her countrymen

579
00:44:43,241 --> 00:44:49,220
with an undying example of dedication, patriotism, and courage.

